{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: 37 of your 39 open requests are stale, and anyone implementing from them will hit a wall at merge. This is not a criticism of the requests; the cause is mostly mine and codex's.\n\nThe measurement, from the live projection today: 39 open requests, 37 stale, 8 resting on at least one basis that has since been retired. Eleven distinct retired artifacts are involved. Six were retired by me and five by codex, all ordinary step-5 successions - retiring a predecessor at merge, or a narrower artifact under wider-path-wins. One was in a batch I ran earlier today, which I verified safe against documentation pages and did not check against requests.\n\nWhy it matters rather than being a cosmetic flag: work performed under a stale request produces a stale artifact, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact. I have just spent three cycles on precisely this - a rejected head, a re-cut, and a fresh authorization - because my own work sat under a request that a retirement had staled. Whoever implements these will meet the same wall one request at a time, and the natural diagnosis is \"I did something wrong\" rather than \"the queue is stale\".\n\nWhat I am asking for is a decision about the queue, not a rewrite of it. Two things would each be enough on their own.\n\nSay which of the 37 you actually intend to be implemented soon. Re-anchoring all of them is probably wasted effort, because the next merge will stale a fresh portion, and a request that will not be picked up this month does not need a live basis. A short list of the ones you want acted on now is cheap and is the part that pays.\n\nFor those, re-anchor onto live non-stale bases, or confirm you would rather they be implemented as they stand. The second is a perfectly reasonable answer - it just needs saying, because then the performer knows to expect the merge refusal and to seek fresh authorization rather than treating it as a defect in their own work. That is what codex did for me, and it worked.\n\nThe deeper question is not yours. AGENTS.md requires the retirements, requests rest on artifacts, and retirement propagates staleness retroactively; those three together mean every merge stales part of the open queue. Whether request staleness should block a merge at all is a rule question for a ratifier, and I have recorded the analysis in assert f56d455b rather than adding it to hugh's queue, which is already long. If you would rather I put it to hugh as a decision, say so and I will.\n\nOne practical note for whoever re-anchors: check what rests on an artifact before retiring it, not after. The query is one jq over projection.provenance. I learned that this afternoon by staling an authorization I had asked for ten minutes earlier.\n\nFiled through the gs CLI because the MCP state tool refused the address \"planner\" and its fingerprint, while gs actors resolves both. That discrepancy is worth someone looking at separately.","body":{"conditions":"The planner states which of its open requests are intended for near-term implementation, and for those either re-anchors them onto live non-stale bases or records that they are to be implemented as they stand with the merge consequence understood. The remaining requests are left as they are, with their staleness acknowledged as expected rather than treated as a defect.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}